<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Permits cleartext (http://) traffic.

  This exists for exactly one reason: the AI-cleanup feature lets the user point
  at an OpenAI-compatible server they run themselves — Ollama, LM Studio or
  llama.cpp on a desktop on the same network — and those serve plain HTTP with no
  practical way to obtain a certificate for a LAN IP. Since Android 9 cleartext
  is blocked by default, and a per-domain allowlist can't be written in advance
  for an address the user types in at runtime.

  The exposure is narrow: the app makes no network requests of its own. The only
  outbound traffic is the request to the endpoint the user configured, and the
  settings screen warns when that endpoint is non-loopback http://. Transcription
  itself remains entirely on-device.

  trust-anchors is deliberately not overridden, so the platform default (system
  CAs) applies to https:// endpoints.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
